      "source": "def merge_intervals(intervals):\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])  # Sort intervals based on start time\n    merged = []\n    for interval in intervals:\n        if not merged or interval[0] > merged[-1][1]:\n            merged.append(interval)\n        else:\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], interval[1])\n    return merged\n",
      "rationale": "The function first handles the empty input case. It then sorts the intervals based on their start times.  It iterates through the sorted intervals, merging overlapping intervals. If the current interval's start time is greater than the end time of the last merged interval, it's a new interval and is added to the merged list. Otherwise, the end time of the last merged interval is updated to be the maximum of its current end time and the end time of the current interval, effectively merging the intervals.",
